  "response": "Read the fixture to ground the review — the helper is at `src/date.ts:51-53`, `return nowMs + 86400000;`.\n\n## The finding\n\n`crime_00019` — **DST-Naive Day Math**, `dst_naive_arithmetic`, `src/date.ts:52`. Severity `medium`, confidence `0.8`, effort `medium`.\n\nEvidence, verbatim:\n\n> \"1 day-level millisecond constant in arithmetic. A day isn't 86,400,000 ms on DST-transition days; manual ms math drifts silently.\"\n>\n> - `\"+ 86400000 (day) @L52\"`\n> - `\"lines: 52\"`\n> - `\"use a calendar-aware library (Luxon, Temporal, date-fns-tz) for day math\"`\n\nFix shape: `\"use a DST-aware library; never add seconds to a wall-clock date\"`. Suggested action `use_calendar_aware_lib`, risk `low`.\n\n## Block or request changes\n\n**Request changes — don't block.** The scan supports that split rather than a hard block:\n\n- Severity is `medium` (0.6), not high, and blast radius is empty (`blast_radius_direct_importers: 0`) — nothing imports this yet, so the defect isn't live in a caller path. A brand-new unimported helper is a cheap thing to fix now and a cheap thing to get wrong later.\n- `test_gap: 0.5` with no `src/date.test.ts` in the repo — nothing pins the behaviour, so \"we'll catch it in CI\" isn't available. The fix must ship with the test.\n- Confidence 0.8 rather than 0.9+ is the honest caveat: **if every consumer treats the result strictly as a UTC instant, `+86_400_000` is exactly correct** — UTC has no DST. What makes it not theoretical in *this* file is `crime_00009` (**Half-UTC, Half-Local**, high/0.85): `\"\\\"d\\\" uses getUTCFullYear() @L9 and getMonth() @L41\"`. The module already reads local wall-clock fields, so a value produced by `tomorrowMs` will meet local-time readers.\n\nSo: request changes on the helper, and make the author state the contract. If `tomorrowMs` means \"the same instant 24 hours later,\" rename it to `plus24hMs` and it's correct as written. If it means \"the same clock time tomorrow,\" it needs `Temporal.ZonedDateTime.add({ days: 1 })` (or Luxon/date-fns-tz) and an explicit zone.\n\nI'd escalate to a block only if a caller uses the result for a calendar-day boundary — billing period ends, trial expiry, digest cutoffs — where an off-by-one-day is user-visible and money-adjacent.\n\n## The two dates per year\n\nThe two **DST transitions in the runtime's local timezone**. There's no zone pinned anywhere in this file, so it's whatever the host is set to:\n\n| Zone | Spring forward (23h day) | Fall back (25h day) |\n|---|---|---|\n| US (`America/New_York`, 2026) | Sun **8 March 2026** | Sun **1 November 2026** |\n| EU (`Europe/London`, 2026) | Sun **29 March 2026** | Sun **25 October 2026** |\n\nHow each one breaks, taking US Eastern:\n\n- **Spring forward.** The local day is 23 hours. `tomorrowMs(2026-03-08T00:00 local)` returns `2026-03-09T01:00` local — an hour past midnight. A \"start of tomorrow\" boundary lands inside the wrong hour, and anything bucketing by that value drops the first hour of 9 March.\n- **Fall back.** The local day is 25 hours. `tomorrowMs(2026-11-01T00:00 local)` returns `2026-11-01T23:00` local — **still the same calendar day**. Tomorrow never arrives; a daily job keyed off this fires twice for 1 November, or a trial gets a free extra hour.\n\nNote the asymmetry: fall-back is the nastier of the two, because the result stays on the same date rather than merely shifting an hour, so date-keyed logic silently double-counts instead of skewing.\n\nTwo smaller notes for the same PR, since the reviewer is already in this file: `crime_00019` sits next to `crime_00013` (**Timezone Roulette**, `\"unsafe literals: \\\"2026-12-25T07:00:00\\\", \\\"2026-12-20\\\"\"`, lines 29, 33) and `crime_00001` (**Temporal Recklessness**, high, `\"1 decide a branch or comparison (lines 4)\"`). If the team is adopting Temporal or Luxon to fix `tomorrowMs`, that's the moment to take the whole file, not just line 52.",
